WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.620
Bases Loaded 2, Second Season.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:07.420
Do you have what it takes to play and manage a World Series team?

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:30.220
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.620
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.400
And I'm Joe.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:41.380
Guys, currently in the baseball universe, we are in like winter meetings time where there's

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:47.420
a bunch of like owners getting together in what I imagine is a log cabin in a really snowy

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.620
place because it's called winter meetings.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.620
Yeah. And they just like exchange fat stacks of cash for players.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.360
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:03.240
Yeah. I just want to say to our listeners real quick, I'm sorry for putting you through some chewing

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.120
gum earlier. We were thinking about making it like we were all managers chewing gum.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.460
I did it.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:10.900
Didn't sound great.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.020
But I'm going to stop now.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:19.920
I appreciate your effort, though, Sean, because this is a game where you are simultaneously the

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.160
players and the manager.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:27.280
Yeah. And it's almost like it's great that, you know, there are these there are these baseball

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.260
games where you only get to play as like half the players at a time.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.320
And there are some baseball games you get to play as all the players at the time.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.380
And this is one of the latter.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:41.040
A little bases loaded retrospective because this is our first baseball sequel game, a little

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.040
refresher, if you will.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.900
Bases loaded. The big appeal in that game for us, at least listening back on the episode, was that it

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:53.320
had a much more realistic looking take on baseball going for the TV style presentation from center

00:01:53.320 --> 00:02:01.580
field with the pitcher on the mound and the batter only facing this, you know, unlike all

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.900
the other baseball games where you then like control from the batter's perspective.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.380
This always stayed in the TV perspective.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:10.520
Bases loaded also had a full season available.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.260
What a full season was, I don't quite remember.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.000
It might have been a shorter season, not a full 162 games.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:18.320
But that was there.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:23.100
And we also commented that the game overall had a bit of a sluggish feel in terms of pace

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:28.600
of play, which would be, you know, a nightmare in today's baseball world because that's all

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.380
they're trying to do is just solve how to make the game faster and faster.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.800
They want these games to be like under two hours.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:39.380
And they just this season coming up, they introduced like a pitch clock so that pitchers have to

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:44.080
throw the ball again within a certain time or else I don't I don't know what happens or

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:45.600
else I didn't look that far into it.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.480
But what will what will the repercussions be?

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:48.500
Can they really do anything?

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.260
Yeah, there's a 10 yard penalty.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.940
Right. The bases come closer to the batters.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:54.200
Second base. Right.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.840
But yeah, I mean, and this sort of sticks with that.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:09.640
What with the bases loaded feel, at least like in that in that hit screen, it still has that

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.880
more realistic viewpoint.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:17.920
Yeah, I'd say bases loaded to is mostly the same game as the original, but with the ability

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:23.840
to manage the players, not only in terms of like building lineups and stuff like that,

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:29.400
but to actually have those players grow and shrink depending on their performance from

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:35.440
game to game kind of creating the real life effect of being hot or being in a slump.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.080
Yeah, not physically grow or shrink, though.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:39.420
Right. Very strange.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.740
That's MLB slug fest for the GameCube.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.580
Joe, any takes on this whole player performance feature?

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.840
So I am always I'm always down for that.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:58.000
I'll be honest. I'm going to need you guys to sort of be my be my eyes and ears as far as like the actual

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:03.980
like roster kind of changing gameplay and like player performance.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.900
I did not play a significant amount of like a season.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:14.180
I played like two games, so I didn't really get to like really see what the like what

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:18.700
what impact that has on like playing a bunch of games or like what impact it has after playing

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:21.100
many games in a row.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:27.660
How you see that like Joe played a good amount of like the career portion or like the management

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.620
portion of this game on a blanket.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.620
Yes, I tried to keep the rosters managed that way.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.340
Yeah, I have a blanket with a grid.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:41.020
I just put up a YouTube video and then I just pretend that I was playing on a dance pad.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.480
Yeah. You know, it's funny, Joe.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:46.560
You only said you said you only played two games.

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:49.840
And so you don't have time to really know anything about the player performance.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:51.940
Not that like, you know, two games an awful long time.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:55.080
It base is loaded. So I'm not saying you didn't give your fair shake here.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:59.840
What I'm saying is, is that when you start this game, the manual doesn't tell you like

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:05.360
there isn't like a cool stats page in the back of the manual for each player and like

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:08.400
what they're good at or like, you know, what their initial ratings are.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:10.280
You boot up the game.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:15.860
You go pick your team and you don't even find out if this team is any good until after you

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:17.120
finish your first game.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.700
Then you're greeted with the stats after that first game.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.280
Yeah, you get like an idea.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:28.460
Like there's, there's something of a, of a presentation where each batter comes to the

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.240
plate and they've got like stats from last year.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:35.500
So you kind of see like, oh, they're, they're a batting percentage is up there.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.540
The amount of home runs they hit is up there.

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:40.200
But like, this isn't based on the game.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:45.380
This is just the game like sort of prescribed these stats to this player.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:49.340
So you get an idea of sort of what their capabilities are from that at least.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:50.420
Yeah, that's a good point.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:56.080
But they, there's an actual like stat for each of those things that is covered inside

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:57.140
the game that you can check.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:02.720
It's different between pitchers and batters, but basically there is a physical sensitivity

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:05.660
and intellectual rating for each person.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:08.360
So for the, I'll break it down here.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:14.820
The pitcher physical means that how long they can go and their control of pitching over the

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:21.520
course of a game. Sensitivity is their speed on the ball and their intellectual rating is how, how much they

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:27.000
can curve the ball, which sounds kind of crazy that it's like, you have to be really smart.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.700
Yeah. You have to be really smart to know how to throw the curve ball.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:32.740
It's all geometry, you know, they do all the geometry in their head.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:39.260
Yeah. And it's funny though, like a really high intellectual player can basically bring the ball all the

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:44.080
way from the top of the plate all the way down to the bottom.

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:48.100
And so it kind of crosses the entire strike zone before landing in the dirt.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:51.600
And I got to imagine that would fool most human players.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:56.460
The computer seems to have a good grip on things, but most human players would probably swing,

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.340
you know, before it reaches the dirt.

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:01.420
I'd say so.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:09.780
Yeah. I mean, even, even not even fully grasping these concepts, I think that the pitching on

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:12.360
the computer end is still pretty good in this game.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:17.100
Yeah. And then for the batters, physical means their contact with the ball.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:19.720
Sensitivity is their power with the bat.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:22.840
And the intellectual is their like this.

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:23.660
I thought this was interesting.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:25.020
It's their clutch factor.

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:30.740
They're basically how, how much better they'll be able to put the ball in play when there

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:34.240
are runners on either second or end or third base.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:35.720
So runners in scoring position.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:40.260
And that's kind of more what I wanted to see in this game is what was described in that

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:45.620
intellectual factor there for the batter is meaningful team building stats, you know,

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:51.800
not just like, oh yeah, this guy, you know, he's, he's tall and he's got, and he's large

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:57.200
in weight. So he's got large power, you know, just like comic book style presentation to that.

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:59.880
And so, yeah, you might be able to hit the ball harder with him.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:06.900
I liked the idea of players being clutch in certain kinds of performances based on where

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:10.280
the rest of their team are and then trying to figure out where you want to slot them into

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:11.360
a lineup accordingly.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:19.920
Yeah. That whole like intangible tangibility, like putting a stat on that is pretty interesting

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:26.660
and like, oh, I mean, I don't really know too much about the, the strategy of baseball.

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:31.760
I only really know what Mike lets me know, but I would imagine you might want to put him

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:36.140
in like the four slot, you know, get a couple of people on base and then he can, he can strike

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:37.940
them in. Right. Beautiful, Sean.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:41.700
Very well said. It sounds like, it sounds like Mike is like withholding knowledge from you.

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:45.080
Like, oh yeah. He's censoring all your, your baseball.

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:46.280
All those Mets games.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:49.040
He just sort of like lets me, I don't need to know basis.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:51.020
Right. Yeah.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:56.740
It's true though. At the end of the day, the most important thing in baseball, in my, you know, my opinion,

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:59.060
there are other people who are going to tell you other stats are very important.

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:01.760
War seems to be a very important stat these days.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:06.900
I would argue that the most important stat in baseball is just your on base percentage.

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:09.160
Does this guy get on base?

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:10.840
And that's like something.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.700
Now is that OBPS or is that OPS?

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:13.920
That's OBP.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:15.780
OBP. Yep.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:18.600
Okay. OPS is a mixture of that and slugging.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:23.040
So, uh, it's slugging is like, you know, when you hit like doubles and so on.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:24.440
Oh yeah. Anything better than that.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:27.780
So this way it's like, wow, not only does this guy get on base, but he fucking crushes

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:32.420
the ball. Um, so yeah, great OPS would be even more impressive.

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:37.860
However, a guy with a high slugging could also have a lot of, um, you know, fly outs

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:39.620
and yeah, strikeouts and stuff like that.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:41.220
And that's not particularly useful either.

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:44.660
So all that to say, none of this really matters in this game.

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:47.720
Like we're talking a lot of like saber metrics and baseball strategy.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:53.400
Now you don't need that in this game because although the ratings are dependent on the particular

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:59.260
player, you, it says in the manual that you can't make somebody who's a light hitter all

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:03.980
of a sudden just become the home run King, uh, because their sensitivity is maxed out.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:08.900
It's just, he'll be for those particular set of for like maybe the next game or so he'll

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:14.360
be a little better at, at hitting that ball a little harder, but the performance is designed

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:18.420
to make the like streaks of, of real life baseball.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:23.600
And I certainly didn't play the game long enough to experience that, but I'd still say

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:28.660
that while that's a really cool concept, it didn't, it didn't affect so much how I was

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:33.020
building my team because of the, probably the limited amount of time I was able to spend

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:34.700
watching this team grow.

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:39.000
So did you guys feel that, I mean, are you saying that you don't, you didn't feel that

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:42.000
it like really kind of did what it was setting out to do?

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:45.660
Cause in my opinion, as someone who didn't play enough to like really experience it,

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:49.780
like this gives you something that some of the other, many of the other baseball games

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:55.640
we've played doesn't, or don't give you because, uh, it gives you like the, gives you incentive

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:59.560
to play a season, you know, rather than just like, okay, you're gonna play a game or you're

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:04.100
gonna play another game. You're just going to play 162 games and they're all going to be roughly the same.

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:08.540
Whereas this, like the managing aspect feels like it would give you the incentive to like,

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:09.800
okay, you're watching your team grow.

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:15.540
You're, you have reason to like keep going forward because like you have progress.

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:22.500
Yeah. I mean, if you're asking me if I played this game long enough to see like that very large,

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:26.800
overarching game mechanic play out, I did not.

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:33.040
Like, I feel like you need to play this for like eight to 10 hours to really get a grasp

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:34.700
on that. Am I wrong?

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:35.880
No, you're not wrong.

00:11:36.020 --> 00:11:39.980
And I would just say, uh, to Joe's question about like, is this meaningful?

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:45.140
I do think it's, it's meaningful because it does change the player performance, which is

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:50.400
much better than just being like, you got to trade players to get better players like that,

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:53.020
you know, just having them be flat all year round.

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:54.800
It wouldn't, wouldn't be as exciting.

00:11:54.960 --> 00:12:01.360
I'd say it's maybe a step below baseball stars system of like being able to train and develop

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:02.420
your players.

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:06.160
Like this is more just based on their performance in that game.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:11.560
And so if, if they had, if they went over four, their stats go down, you know, and that

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:17.760
does make sense and, and translates, but it's, it's not as, um, it's not as deep or as rich.

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:24.020
It's just based on how good you are as a player, not necessarily, you know, your entire team,

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:27.240
like kind of being like, Oh, well this guy is getting hot, but this guy is cold.

00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:32.740
So if this game, if you played this game for long enough and you never personally improved,

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:37.280
you might just like bottom out an entire professional baseball team.

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:40.260
Like it's just a self-fulfilling prophecy.

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:43.720
Yes. If you're bad at the game and now granted, they also make it this way.

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:49.740
Even if your physical stat is like as low as possible, it doesn't mean that you're never

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:51.260
going to be able to make contact with the ball.

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:54.840
It was the, the guys who have great natural contact stats.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:57.140
It just, it just fluctuates them slightly.

00:12:57.140 --> 00:12:58.380
And I feel like it's still cool.

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:04.080
And since this is the meatiest addition to the game, I, I guess that's why we're spending

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:05.100
so much time on it.

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:08.340
But you know, there's other things about this game that have changed too.

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:12.160
There's a, there's a few camera differences and I'd like to know from you guys, whether

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:14.420
you thought these helped or hurt the game.

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:24.240
Yeah. Um, I think that in terms of fielding and making it not quite as unwieldy as some previous games

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:29.780
that we've played, this was a, a huge, a huge like addition to the game.

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:37.940
Like, uh, the, the angle it's either from like the, the first baseline or the third baseline,

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:41.680
depending on who, who's on defense at the time.

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:48.960
And it seems to be that like perfect middle ground of wide enough to know where things

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:54.760
are going and sort of have an idea of where your fielders are and close enough to have

00:13:54.760 --> 00:14:00.980
some kind of control over where you're throwing and like where you're jumping.

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:05.000
And I just really liked the way that fielding worked in this game.

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:10.260
Yeah. I felt like, uh, fielding in this felt, I mean, maybe I'm misremembering some of the other

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:13.820
baseball games, but it felt the most intuitive to control.

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:18.040
It felt the smoothest and the most like I'm in one-to-one control of the players.

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:22.940
Well, a large problem we have with fielding in a lot of the other baseball games is the

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:28.040
way that you don't see your outfielders, which is arguably a very important position in

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:32.300
the game where people are going to, uh, hit pop-ups that should be easy outs.

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:36.560
But if you can't see your outfielders, you might not be able to get to the ball where

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:38.500
the easy out is in time to catch it.

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:42.600
And then that really affects not only the pace of play, but your ability to win games.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:49.460
This something about the speed of the ball in play, and they did change the point of view

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:54.320
of where, uh, of how they show the ball that's different than bases loaded one.

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:57.380
Something about that I thought was a little easier to register as well.

00:14:57.380 --> 00:15:03.060
There's still going to be line drives that would be impossible to catch unless you, uh,

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:07.560
you know, you kind of knew where it was coming, or if you saw the play enough, maybe you'd be

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:11.420
able to like perfect a, a dive, uh, in a particular direction.

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:13.500
I don't, I don't, I can't confirm if that's possible.

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:17.480
I'm just saying maybe over time you would learn something like that, but being able to make

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:23.500
routine plays and understand, um, controls was very straightforward in this game.

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:24.900
And I thought that really helped.

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:34.220
Yeah, I think just that, that 25 degree or 45 degree shift for the point of view, uh, really

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:38.680
helped just like have your mental map a bit clearer.

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:43.440
I mean, it's not, it's not crazy to think like if you're, if you're at the home base,

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:47.740
if you're at home plate looking out into the field, like you should know where your field

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:55.200
is. Sorry, that's pretty simple, but that shift, like it clarified in my mental map where everybody

00:15:55.200 --> 00:16:02.640
should be. So I wasn't like preemptively moving my fielder in the wrong direction for too long to make

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:06.560
the play a triple instead of a double or a double instead of a single.

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:09.300
It seemed to be a bit more intuitive in that matter.

