And that is that the, for the Washington DC team, all of the players are named after politicians

00:31:51.100 --> 00:31:52.980
in Los Angeles.

00:31:52.980 --> 00:31:56.500
All the players take their names from Hollywood stars.

00:31:56.920 --> 00:32:00.520
And I think there was one other that I can't remember.

00:32:00.740 --> 00:32:02.480
Oh, it's in the Japanese game.

00:32:02.980 --> 00:32:05.940
They did something with like the developers or something like that.

00:32:07.500 --> 00:32:12.900
I mean, yeah, baseball stars definitely made me not care as much

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:19.880
about that. I still think that it's, it's weird when it's just a couple teams that get like special

00:32:19.880 --> 00:32:25.360
treatment. I guess you can count that as an Easter egg, but like, how come, you know, Minnesota doesn't

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:29.060
have a bunch of Minnesotans on it that like famous Minnesotans, you know?

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:30.880
Yeah, all those.

00:32:31.040 --> 00:32:32.600
Or maybe I'm thinking of Iowa.

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:36.500
I don't know. But yeah, it's cute.

00:32:36.860 --> 00:32:39.500
I would just say it'd be tough to do that for every city.

00:32:39.700 --> 00:32:49.720
I know. The original Bases Loaded sold 1.5 million copies.

00:32:50.160 --> 00:32:54.600
And there's nothing to suggest that Bases Loaded 2 didn't sell as well, if not better.

00:32:54.880 --> 00:32:59.460
They're still going to make two more sequels of this game, Bases Loaded 3 and 4.

00:33:00.080 --> 00:33:03.040
And you can pick this card up today for just five bucks.

00:33:03.280 --> 00:33:07.180
So chances are it sold really well for it to be that cheap of an NES game.

00:33:07.180 --> 00:33:15.280
Yeah, I guess this is where we start to see franchise loyalty kick in, maybe?

00:33:15.620 --> 00:33:16.780
I don't know. Right.

00:33:16.940 --> 00:33:19.980
You bought Bases Loaded 1, so it's like, you're going to buy Bases Loaded 2.

00:33:20.040 --> 00:33:23.820
I couldn't find actual numbers for how much Bases Loaded 2 sold, but obviously it sold

00:33:23.820 --> 00:33:27.240
well for them to say, yeah, we're going to make another one and another one, and then we're

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:28.820
going to make more for the Super Nintendo.

00:33:28.820 --> 00:33:34.640
I know it's funny how many baseball games there are, though, on the Nintendo Entertainment

00:33:34.640 --> 00:33:38.060
System. Not even just sequels of games, but just competing.

00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:41.980
These games we played already. Yeah, just competing games because you don't have that anymore.

00:33:42.220 --> 00:33:45.160
It's either you play MLB The Show or you're screwed.

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:46.980
Yeah. Right?

00:33:47.120 --> 00:33:49.140
Like, does Madden have any competition?

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:53.480
No, they signed an exclusive contract with the NFL.

00:33:54.500 --> 00:33:57.680
And I don't know if the show did as well with MLB.

00:33:57.820 --> 00:34:04.760
I just know that I was under the assumption that EA was going on a hiatus with baseball,

00:34:05.400 --> 00:34:09.040
but I don't remember the last time EA released a baseball game.

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:13.620
Yeah, the last one I could think of is back in 2005, MVP Baseball.

00:34:13.820 --> 00:34:14.660
That's the GOAT.

00:34:16.040 --> 00:34:17.740
By a lot of people, not just me.

00:34:17.740 --> 00:34:22.920
It is considered, yeah, MVP Baseball 2005 with Manny Ramirez on the cover is considered.

00:34:23.540 --> 00:34:27.800
That game let you, you talk about being a manager of your team in Bases Loaded 2.

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:33.020
In that game, you were like the owner of the actual team and could set the price of the

00:34:33.020 --> 00:34:34.240
popcorn in the stadium.

00:34:34.240 --> 00:34:36.940
Okay, yeah. So this is like rollercoaster tycoon.

00:34:37.240 --> 00:34:39.400
Yes, yeah. It had a whole other mode to it.

00:34:39.840 --> 00:34:44.540
Yeah, that sounds like late 2000s Madden as well.

00:34:44.540 --> 00:34:51.560
I think they kind of got rid of those things or at least reduced how important they are to your financial well-being.

00:34:51.840 --> 00:35:00.720
But it also sounds like the year 2005 was just the high watermark for alternative sports games

00:35:00.720 --> 00:35:04.540
because, you know, that's also ESPN NFL 2K5.

00:35:04.540 --> 00:35:11.560
So that was, I gotta start playing more 2005 games.

00:35:12.280 --> 00:35:17.740
This game, believe it or not, was novelized as part of the Worlds of Power series.

00:35:17.980 --> 00:35:23.160
You know, the books that we've been covering for like Ninja Gaiden and Blaster Master.

00:35:23.560 --> 00:35:25.260
Well, Bases Loaded 2.

00:35:25.580 --> 00:35:27.020
Not Bases Loaded 1, keep in mind.

00:35:27.220 --> 00:35:32.580
But for this sequel, they were like, we really need to lock down a Worlds of Power book for this one.

00:35:32.580 --> 00:35:39.220
And it's a really weird pick, and I didn't look up the plot or anything, but from the author's website,

00:35:40.140 --> 00:35:43.480
he says that since the game itself didn't have much of a plot,

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:47.620
he was free to create a comedic baseball storyline,

00:35:48.600 --> 00:35:51.900
and it turned out to be the title that he felt was the most fun to write

00:35:51.900 --> 00:35:56.660
because all the other ones he kind of had to adhere to the video game plot.

00:35:56.880 --> 00:36:00.000
Well, I can't wait until we read that for our listeners.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:03.280
Yeah, if you want to see content like that, just let us know.

00:36:03.480 --> 00:36:07.980
We'll do a whole Worlds of Power series and then get cease and desist letters in the mail.

00:36:10.160 --> 00:36:15.980
One thing that we don't do by mail, but we do on the podcast, is the Essential Games list.

00:36:18.760 --> 00:36:22.780
Sean, your vote for Bases Loaded 2, second season.

00:36:23.080 --> 00:36:24.700
Oh, it is still December, isn't it?

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:26.140
Yeah. Yep.

00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:30.000
This is one of my final votes.

00:36:30.260 --> 00:36:31.940
First. My final first vote.

00:36:32.880 --> 00:36:36.860
Yeah, so we put Baseball Stars on the Essential Games list,

00:36:36.880 --> 00:36:39.120
and then we took it off not too long ago.

00:36:40.340 --> 00:36:44.220
And as much as I think this is a very competent baseball game,

00:36:44.920 --> 00:36:49.740
and a lot of the mechanics that they're adding and changing, I like,

00:36:50.540 --> 00:36:58.300
but if I had to take off Baseball Stars with all of its fun amusements,

00:36:58.960 --> 00:37:03.840
the metagame stuff that seemed like no one else was doing that,

00:37:04.360 --> 00:37:09.660
I can't in good conscience put Bases Loaded 2, second season on here.

00:37:09.820 --> 00:37:14.540
I think it's very good, but it doesn't have the personality that that has,

00:37:14.600 --> 00:37:18.400
and in sports games that's very important, so not essential.

00:37:18.400 --> 00:37:25.280
Joe? I think we use this argument of, like,

00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:27.180
if something better comes along,

00:37:27.640 --> 00:37:32.340
it doesn't mean that the earlier thing is still not, doesn't have value.

00:37:32.540 --> 00:37:35.760
And then we also use the argument that, like, hey, this is better,

00:37:35.780 --> 00:37:38.660
so I don't need to play this one anymore.

00:37:38.860 --> 00:37:40.040
Like, we use those both.

00:37:40.040 --> 00:37:41.200
I think we did that in the best of,

00:37:41.220 --> 00:37:44.080
I think we talked about both those things in the best of 89.

00:37:44.080 --> 00:37:47.500
And I just want to clear that up with this vote,

00:37:48.120 --> 00:37:52.200
where I think that there's a difference for me with, like, a baseball game,

00:37:52.220 --> 00:37:55.600
where something comes along in the future.

00:37:55.820 --> 00:37:58.800
I know plenty of baseball games will come along after this,

00:38:00.460 --> 00:38:02.900
that will make me realize, like,

00:38:03.140 --> 00:38:06.600
okay, this has improved the whole formula of this,

00:38:06.600 --> 00:38:11.860
and there's nothing left in that older baseball game that makes me need to go back to it.

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:13.940
And then there are other games where it's like,

00:38:14.020 --> 00:38:15.300
oh, this has improved the whole formula,

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:18.800
but there's still value.

00:38:19.080 --> 00:38:21.380
I don't want to say value, like there's no value in this game,

00:38:21.480 --> 00:38:23.780
but, like, there's still something about Super Mario Brothers

00:38:24.940 --> 00:38:29.500
that is fantastic in the gameplay and in the charm and in the environments and in the levels

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:31.680
that, like, just because Super Mario Brothers 3 exists,

00:38:31.860 --> 00:38:32.840
I don't have to go back to it.

00:38:32.840 --> 00:38:39.220
But with a game like baseball, it's hard for me to feel that way about it,

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:43.060
because it's like a lot of them are trying to do the same thing.

00:38:43.340 --> 00:38:46.700
So when one improves on the earlier iteration,

00:38:47.120 --> 00:38:49.680
it is hard for me to say, like, yeah, this is an essential game.

00:38:49.780 --> 00:38:53.920
Like, if you want to play a baseball game, it's got to be this one,

00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:55.380
or, like, this has to be one of them.

00:38:55.600 --> 00:38:56.780
So I'm going to say no.

00:38:56.780 --> 00:39:00.760
I would say that this could be maybe an essential NES baseball game

00:39:00.760 --> 00:39:02.840
of all the NES baseball games,

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:06.840
but I just think, you know, go play MVP 2005.

00:39:06.840 --> 00:39:10.640
I hear that's a good baseball experience.

00:39:10.880 --> 00:39:13.000
Rumor has it, that's all I let Sean know.

00:39:13.620 --> 00:39:15.420
Yeah. No, yes, exactly.

00:39:15.420 --> 00:39:18.300
You know, I love the idea of players getting hot

00:39:18.300 --> 00:39:20.960
and going into slumps and trying to just do something

00:39:20.960 --> 00:39:24.180
to emulate the season experience.

00:39:25.220 --> 00:39:27.700
Everybody who's a fan of any particular baseball team

00:39:27.700 --> 00:39:31.140
will note, like, my favorite player is my favorite when they're good.

00:39:31.320 --> 00:39:33.500
And it's like, then they start to, you know,

00:39:33.940 --> 00:39:36.760
have, like, four strikeout games in a row,

00:39:36.780 --> 00:39:38.720
and you're, like, start booing them,

00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:40.320
and then you love them again when they get hot.

00:39:40.320 --> 00:39:44.400
So it feels like if this was the baseball game your family bought

00:39:44.880 --> 00:39:46.580
and that was all you had,

00:39:46.640 --> 00:39:49.260
or at least until bases loaded three, right,

00:39:49.400 --> 00:39:51.580
you could easily sink a ton of time into it

00:39:51.580 --> 00:39:53.560
and try to get far into a season.

00:39:53.680 --> 00:39:55.160
I'm not saying you're going to complete the whole season

00:39:55.160 --> 00:39:57.580
and questioning if that's even worth it

00:39:57.580 --> 00:40:00.060
because you get a screen that says world champions

00:40:00.060 --> 00:40:02.760
followed by some credits and then a team photo,

00:40:02.760 --> 00:40:05.360
and the team photo is stock.

00:40:05.560 --> 00:40:07.320
You know, it's not like your experience

00:40:07.320 --> 00:40:09.980
that experience across the 130 games

00:40:09.980 --> 00:40:11.500
changes what happens in the photo.

00:40:11.760 --> 00:40:13.500
So not sure how much that's worth it.

00:40:13.600 --> 00:40:15.900
My point being, wrapping it back up to my own childhood,

00:40:16.660 --> 00:40:17.760
growing up on the N64,

00:40:18.400 --> 00:40:21.040
we only had MLB featuring Ken Griffey Jr.

00:40:21.520 --> 00:40:23.720
And between me, my dad, and my brother,

00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:26.160
we all took our chances at playing a full season.

00:40:26.660 --> 00:40:28.480
And I think the closest we ever got was,

00:40:28.540 --> 00:40:29.960
and it was 162-game season,

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:32.000
the closest we ever got was, like, 120 games in.

00:40:32.160 --> 00:40:33.340
And then you just, like, I don't know,

00:40:33.380 --> 00:40:35.020
you get tired of it, you stop playing for a little bit,

00:40:35.080 --> 00:40:37.100
you forget what happened, and you just start a new season.

00:40:37.100 --> 00:40:39.060
I never got to the end of a season,

00:40:39.180 --> 00:40:40.780
and I never felt like, oh, this game stinks

00:40:40.780 --> 00:40:42.600
because I never got all the way to the end.

00:40:42.740 --> 00:40:44.480
But that's really the only reason

00:40:44.480 --> 00:40:45.980
you would go through a full season here.

00:40:46.140 --> 00:40:48.120
There isn't actually enough reward

00:40:48.120 --> 00:40:50.840
or dynamic changes across the season

00:40:50.840 --> 00:40:52.120
to make it worth it.

00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:54.400
And for a game that's all about selling you,

00:40:54.480 --> 00:40:56.080
I mean, the first thing in the back of the box was,

00:40:56.240 --> 00:40:58.800
can you play and manage a World Series team?

00:40:59.140 --> 00:41:00.720
For it to focus on that,

00:41:01.140 --> 00:41:04.440
they mostly focused on the same repetitive experiences

00:41:04.440 --> 00:41:06.720
that you'll have over and over and over again.

00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:09.240
And for that, it doesn't really stand out too much,

00:41:09.280 --> 00:41:11.400
other than the little details we mentioned,

00:41:11.840 --> 00:41:13.680
against all the other baseball games we played on the NES.

00:41:13.780 --> 00:41:15.260
So I don't think it's enough to make it pop.

00:41:15.660 --> 00:41:18.200
Are you a bad enough dude to win the World Series?

